WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.868
-Hi, everyone.
We were on hiatus last week.

00:00:02.868 --> 00:00:04.870
And I want to start tonight
by saying

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.239
how happy and lucky I feel
to be back with you,

00:00:07.239 --> 00:00:10.577
as well as how upset I am
by this incredibly sad moment

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:12.445
of American history
we're all living through.

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:14.080
Many events brought us here,

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.082
but the focus is the brutal
killing of George Floyd,

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:18.951
a black man, at the hands
of a Minnesota policeman.

00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:20.820
As a white man, I can't speak

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.424
of the deep-rooted and justified
fear African-Americans have

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:26.159
when encountered by police,

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:29.895
so here is "Late Night" writer
Amber Ruffin.

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:31.531
-Let me tell you a story.

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:35.868
So, once when I was a teenager,
I was brand-new to driving,

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:38.337
and I dropped my friend off
at work.

00:00:38.337 --> 00:00:40.073
And I'm coming back home,

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:42.175
and I'm in morning
rush-hour traffic,

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:44.911
and everybody is going
over the speed limit.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:48.381
I think it's like 40,
and everyone is going 50.

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:51.183
And I hate it
because I'm new to driving.

00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:52.685
It's too scary.

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:56.990
When I slow down, it disrupts
traffic, so I speed up to 45.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:58.425
That's five miles
over the speed limit.

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:00.693
That's as fast
as I'm willing to go.

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:02.628
And I know I'm going too slow,

00:01:02.628 --> 00:01:04.831
and I'm in an unfamiliar
part of town

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:06.666
and people are whizzing by me.

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:10.470
So to make myself feel better,
I turn on Busta Rhymes,

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:15.074
and I friggin' blast it so that,
you know, I can calm down.

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:18.111
And blasting Busta Rhymes
is something that I recommend

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:21.380
if you're a teenager
who is unsure of themselves.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.417
So, I get to a good pace,
and I start feeling normal.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:27.821
And just then,
I encounter a speed trap.

00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:30.189
And no one is slowing down.

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:34.193
We are all speeding,
me least of all.

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:38.497
So, to my right,
there is an old, white cop

00:01:38.497 --> 00:01:40.099
standing on
the side of the road.

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:43.903
And out of these tens of cars,
he sees a young, black person

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:48.407
driving a purple car
blasting rap music.

00:01:48.407 --> 00:01:51.710
And he chooses me.
And he's screaming at me.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:55.114
He is shouting,
as if I have murdered someone.

00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:58.818
Like, on a scale of 1 to 10,
he is at a 27.

00:01:58.818 --> 00:02:01.687
And he goes, "Pull the car over!

00:02:01.687 --> 00:02:05.758
Pull the goddamn car over
right now, mother [bleep]!"

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:07.961
That is what this cop
is screaming at me,

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:12.898
and I think, "This is how I die.
This man is going to kill me."

00:02:12.898 --> 00:02:15.502
And I start crying.

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:19.404
I am bawling,
because I am 100% sure

00:02:19.404 --> 00:02:21.674
that this man is going to

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:24.244
drag me out of my car,
beat me to death,

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:27.546
and, you know, tomorrow on the
news, everyone will be like,

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:30.917
"She didn't seem angry,
but who knows?"

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:33.786
So, I pull over, and I stop.

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:37.323
He goes, "Pull over
the [bleep] car! [Bleep]!"

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:39.592
He's shouting. It does not stop.

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:43.595
So, he gets up to the car, and
he puts his hand on the door.

00:02:43.595 --> 00:02:46.598
And he goes to start yelling,
and he sees me.

00:02:46.598 --> 00:02:52.338
He sees a teenage girl
whose face is wet with tears.

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:53.840
And I'm just braced,

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.475
trying to think of all the good
things that have happened

00:02:56.475 --> 00:02:58.678
in my life so that
I get to heaven thankful,

00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:00.113
instead of angry.

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:02.048
And he looks me in the eye.

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:07.520
And he drops it and goes,
"Oh, okay, well, just --

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.356
It's okay.
Just let me have your I.D."

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:11.991
And, you know, he's taken aback.

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:14.827
He goes from a 27
to a negative 2.

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:19.799
His whole demeanor changes,
and it's as if

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:25.104
he wasn't the guy
who was just screaming at me.

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:27.440
He's a totally different guy.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:28.907
All of a sudden, he's nice.

00:03:28.907 --> 00:03:34.446
So I give him my I.D.

00:03:34.446 --> 00:03:37.483
He sends me on my way
without a warning,

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:41.955
because once he saw
a teenage girl...

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:44.457
shouting was no longer fun?

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:46.993
Look, I have a thousand stories
like this.

00:03:46.993 --> 00:03:49.362
The cops have pulled a gun
on me.

00:03:49.362 --> 00:03:52.832
The cops have followed me
to my own home.

00:03:52.832 --> 00:03:59.371
And every black person I know
has a few stories like that.

00:03:59.371 --> 00:04:02.575
Many have more than a few.

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:05.077
Black people
leave the house every day

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:07.313
knowing that at any time,

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:09.515
we could get murdered
by the police.

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:11.050
It's a lot.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:14.586
And sometimes
when you see news footage

00:04:14.586 --> 00:04:16.555
like we have seen the past week

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:20.259
and you hear people chalking
it up to a few bad apples

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:22.728
instead of how corrupt
an entire system is,

00:04:22.728 --> 00:04:25.765
it becomes too much.

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:29.569
And that's what I wanted to say.

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:31.537
And I wanted to end this
with something hopeful

00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:33.439
to, you know,
provide some comfort,

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:37.577
but maybe it's time
to get uncomfortable.

